---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro'
import Chat from '../components/Chat.jsx'
import { chapters } from '../data/chapters.js'

// chapters that are written so far get links
const written = new Set(Object.keys(import.meta.glob('./*.mdx')).map(p => p.slice(2, -4)))
const first = chapters.find(c => written.has(c.slug))
---
<Base>
  <section class="prose intro">
    <h1>A chat model I wrote in assembly</h1>
    <p>
      Mnemonic is a small language model that I wrote by hand in x86-64 assembly and in PTX, the assembly language of
      NVIDIA's GPUs. That covers everything it took to make it: reading the training data, the tokenizer, the training
      on the GPU, shrinking the weights afterwards, and the program you chat with. None of it is C or Python, and it
      doesn't use the CUDA toolkit. The only outside code it calls is the Windows API and the NVIDIA driver.
    </p>
    <p>
      I trained it at home on an RTX 5070 Ti, which is a graphics card made for games. It's nowhere near the models
      people usually chat with, but it knows its name, keeps up a simple conversation and explains everyday things
      reasonably well.
    </p>
    <p>
      You can try it further down this page. I built it in eleven stages and wrote a chapter about each one, starting
      with {first ? <a href={`/${first.slug}`}>{first.title.toLowerCase()}</a> : 'the chapters below'}. The code is on <a href="https://github.com/Supergoatscriptguy/Mnemonic">GitHub</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="numbers">
    <p class="prose">This is what went into the current version.</p>
    <dl class="facts">
      <dt>Parameters</dt>
      <dd>304 million, in 24 layers</dd>
      <dt>Training data</dt>
      <dd>10 billion tokens of FineWeb-Edu, then 389 million tokens of chat data</dd>
      <dt>Training time</dt>
      <dd>90.4 hours of pretraining and 3.4 hours of chat tuning, on one RTX 5070 Ti</dd>
      <dt>Training speed</dt>
      <dd>about 32,000 tokens a second, 68 TFLOPS in bf16</dd>
      <dt>Chat speed on a CPU</dt>
      <dd>about 220 tokens a second with int8 weights, on 12 threads of a Core Ultra 7 265F</dd>
      <dt>The browser engine</dt>
      <dd>9,867 bytes of WebAssembly</dd>
    </dl>
  </section>

  <section class="prose" id="try">
    <h2>Try it</h2>
    <p>
      It runs in your browser on your own computer, so the model has to download before it can answer. It handles
      small talk and simple explanations, but it often gets facts and arithmetic wrong, and it does better with one
      question at a time than with a long back and forth.
    </p>
    <Chat client:load />
  </section>

  <section class="prose" id="chapters">
    <h2>How it was built</h2>
    <ul class="toc">
      {chapters.map(c => (
        <li>
          {written.has(c.slug) ? <a href={`/${c.slug}`}>{c.title}</a> : <span>{c.title} [TODO]</span>}
          <span class="what">{c.summary}</span>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  </section>

  <div class="prose outro">
    <p>If you'd rather run it on your own machine, Ollama has the 300M:</p>
    <pre><code>ollama run supergoatscriptguy/mnemonic</code></pre>
    <p>The weights for its own CPU engine are on <a href="https://huggingface.co/SuperGoatScriptGuy/Mnemonic">Hugging Face</a>.</p>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .intro {
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
  }
  .numbers {
    margin-top: 3.5rem;
  }
  .numbers p {
    margin-bottom: 0.6rem;
  }
  #try {
    margin-top: 5rem;
  }
  #chapters {
    margin-top: 4.5rem;
  }
  .toc {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
  }
  .toc li {
    margin: 0 0 0.9rem;
  }
  .toc .what {
    display: block;
    color: var(--muted);
  }
  .outro {
    margin-top: 3.5rem;
  }
</style>
